    <p>
      I listened the next morning.
    </p>

    <p>
      And it made me stop for a moment.
    </p>

    <p>
      Which was inconvenient.
    </p>

    <p>
      Because whenever something became remotely sincere, one of us seemed to have an
      almost biological need to throw something else into the conversation.
    </p>

    <p>
      So naturally, biltong.
    </p>

    <p>
      Dry.
    </p>

    <p>
      Wet.
    </p>

    <p>
      Somewhere in the middle.
    </p>

    <p>
      And then there was the question of being easy.
    </p>

    <p>
      You were easy.
    </p>

    <p>
      I had already worked out that easy came with an entry fee.
    </p>

    <p>
      Important information.
    </p>

    <p>
      We were learning about each other.
    </p>

    <p>
      One inappropriate tangent at a time.
    </p>

    <p>
      And then the future started sneaking into the conversation too.
    </p>

    <p>
      Not in a big, scary <em>THE FUTURE</em> kind of way.
    </p>

    <p>
      Just little things.
    </p>

    <p>
      Things we'd do.
    </p>

    <p>
      Places we'd go.
    </p>

    <p>
      Cape Town appeared as a possibility.
    </p>

    <p>
      A casual little <em>we should.</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      And those are dangerous words when you're enjoying someone this much.
    </p>

    <p>
      Because suddenly the person you're getting to know is also appearing in things
      that haven't happened yet.
    </p>

    <p>
      And then it was Saturday.
    </p>

    <p>
      Padel first.
    </p>

    <p>
      You and rugby later.
    </p>

    <p>
      Zero rugby was watched on this specific day.
    </p>

    <p>
      I did not care.
    </p>

    <p>
      Not even a little bit.
    </p>

    <p>
      I was far too busy being excited about you.
    </p>

    <p>
      Experiments were done.
    </p>

    <p>
      For science, of course.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sneaky touches krept in, and you kept disappearing into the corner of the couch.
    </p>

    <p>
      Science tried harder.
    </p>

    <p>
      Became better.
    </p>

    <p>
      And eventually succeeded.
    </p>

    <p>
      We spoke about time, the importance of it and the <em>ella</em> of not wanting
      to waste it.
    </p>

    <p>
      You took my hand.
    </p>

    <p>
      But in reality, you took so much more.
    </p>

    <p>
      A monkey was born that night.
    </p>

    <p>
      Right after I fell off the couch.
    </p>

    <p>
      Traumatic birth, really.
    </p>

    <p>
      And Sunday slow days became my favourite.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>After the first one.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      Because <strong>YOU.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      Then came the next stage of the DHHMT.
    </p>

    <p>
      New definitions.
    </p>

    <p>
      New categories.
    </p>

    <p>
      A whole taxonomy was developing.
    </p>

